---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import "../../styles/editorial-pages.css";
---

<BaseLayout
	title="For authors — Theoria"
	description="Instructions for authors, submission requirements, citation style, peer review and editorial policies of Theoria"
	lang="en"
>
	<main class="editorial-page">
		<section class="heading">
			<p class="label">THEORIA · FOR AUTHORS</p>

			<h1>For authors</h1>

			<p class="lead">
				Instructions for preparing and submitting manuscripts,
				citation rules, peer-review procedure and the principal
				editorial policies of <em>Theoria</em>.
			</p>
		</section>

		<section class="quick-info" aria-label="Key information">
			<div>
				<p class="label">LENGTH</p>
				<p class="value">Up to 35,000 characters</p>
				<p class="small">including spaces · maximum 1.5 author’s sheets</p>
			</div>

			<div>
				<p class="label">PEER REVIEW</p>
				<p class="value">Double-blind</p>
				<p class="small">at least two independent reviewers</p>
			</div>

			<div>
				<p class="label">FEES</p>
				<p class="value">No fees</p>
				<p class="small">submission, processing and publication are free</p>
			</div>
		</section>

		<section class="section" id="submission">
			<p class="label">SUBMISSION</p>

			<div class="prose">
				<h2>What Theoria publishes</h2>

				<p>
					<em>Theoria</em> is published four times a year — in March,
					June, September and December. The journal publishes original
					scholarly articles in philosophy, primarily in Serbian and
					English, as well as review articles, translations of
					philosophical texts, critical discussions of philosophical
					works and reviews of philosophical books.
				</p>

				<p>
					Texts in Serbian are submitted and published in the Latin
					script. All manuscripts undergo a double-blind peer-review
					procedure.
				</p>

				<p>
					A manuscript must constitute the authors’ original
					contribution, must not have been published previously and
					must not be under consideration for publication elsewhere.
				</p>
			</div>
		</section>

		<section class="section" id="technical-requirements">
			<p class="label">MANUSCRIPT PREPARATION</p>

			<div class="prose">
				<h2>Structure and technical requirements</h2>

				<p>
					The author’s first and last name should appear in the upper
					left-hand corner before the title of the paper. The title
					should be written in capital letters.
				</p>

				<p>
					The title should be followed by an abstract of
					<strong>80–150 words</strong> and
					<strong>3–8 keywords</strong>. At the end of the main text,
					before the bibliography, the author’s first and last name
					and institutional or other affiliation should be stated.
				</p>

				<p>
					If the paper is written in Serbian, the author’s name,
					paper title, summary and keywords in English should appear
					after the bibliography. If the paper is written in English,
					the corresponding information should be provided in
					Serbian.
				</p>

				<p>
					Notes should appear as footnotes rather than endnotes.
					Italics should be used for emphasis rather than bold type,
					except for section headings and subheadings.
				</p>

				<p>
					In papers written in Serbian, foreign authors’ names should
					be transliterated, and on first occurrence the name should
					also be given in its original form in parentheses.
				</p>

				<h2>File format</h2>

				<ul>
					<li>
						Microsoft Word document:
						<code>.doc</code> or <code>.docx</code>;
					</li>
					<li>Times New Roman, 12 pt;</li>
					<li>1.5 line spacing;</li>
					<li>no page numbering;</li>
					<li>
						maximum 35,000 characters including spaces, i.e.
						up to 1.5 author’s sheets.
					</li>
				</ul>
			</div>
		</section>

		<section class="section" id="citation">
			<p class="label">CITATION STYLE</p>

			<div class="prose">
				<h2>References</h2>

				<p>
					Full bibliographic details should be given in the
					reference list. In the main text, the shortened form
					<strong>(author year: page)</strong> should be used.
				</p>

				<div class="example">
					<p class="example-label">BOOK</p>

					<p>
						Jorati, Julia (2017).
						<em>Leibniz on Causation and Agency</em>
						(Cambridge: Cambridge University Press).
					</p>

					<p class="citation-example">
						In text: (Jorati 2017: 43)
					</p>
				</div>

				<div class="example">
					<p class="example-label">BOOK IN SERBIAN</p>

					<p>
						Cekić, Nenad (2019).
						<em>Utilitarizam i Bentamova filozofija morala</em>
						(Novi Sad: Akademska knjiga).
					</p>

					<p class="citation-example">
						In text: (Cekić 2019: 54)
					</p>
				</div>

				<div class="example">
					<p class="example-label">JOURNAL ARTICLE</p>

					<p>
						Tsouna, Voula (2016). “Epicurean Preconceptions,”
						<em>Phronesis</em> 61: 160–221.
					</p>

					<p class="citation-example">
						In text: (Tsouna 2016: 169)
					</p>
				</div>

				<div class="example">
					<p class="example-label">ARTICLE IN THEORIA</p>

					<p>
						Ognjanović, Zoran (2025). “The Leibniz’s Role in the
						Development of the Theory of Probability,”
						<em>Theoria</em> 68(4): 6–19.
					</p>

					<p class="citation-example">
						In text: (Ognjanović 2025: 11)
					</p>
				</div>

				<div class="example">
					<p class="example-label">CHAPTER IN AN EDITED VOLUME</p>

					<p>
						Bennett, Jonathan (1996). “Spinoza's Metaphysics,”
						in Don Garrett (ed.),
						<em>The Cambridge Companion to Spinoza</em>
						(Cambridge: Cambridge University Press): 61–88.
					</p>

					<p class="citation-example">
						In text: (Bennett 1996: 87)
					</p>
				</div>

				<div class="example">
					<p class="example-label">CHAPTER IN SERBIAN</p>

					<p>
						Frege, Gotlob (1992). “O smislu i nominatumu,”
						in Aleksandar Pavković and Živan Lazović (eds.),
						<em>Ogledi o jeziku i značenju</em>
						(Beograd: FDS): 35–49.
					</p>

					<p class="citation-example">
						In text: (Frege 1992: 35)
					</p>
				</div>

				<div class="example">
					<p class="example-label">ONLINE SOURCE</p>

					<p>
						Ridge, Michael (2019). “Moral Non-Naturalism,”
						<em>The Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy</em>,
						Edward N. Zalta (ed.),
						https://plato.stanford.edu/archives/fall2019/entries/moral-non-naturalism
						(20 August 2019).
					</p>

					<p class="citation-example">
						In text: (Ridge 2019)
					</p>
				</div>
			</div>
		</section>

		<section class="section" id="before-submission">
			<p class="label">BEFORE SUBMISSION</p>

			<div class="prose">
				<h2>Authors’ responsibilities</h2>

				<p>
					Before submitting a manuscript, authors should make sure
					that the following conditions have been met:
				</p>

				<ul>
					<li>
						the manuscript is original, has not been published
						previously and is not under consideration elsewhere;
					</li>

					<li>
						all and only those who made a significant contribution
						to the paper are listed as co-authors;
					</li>

					<li>
						the contributions of other persons are acknowledged in
						an appropriate note or acknowledgements section;
					</li>

					<li>
						if the work was produced within a research project,
						the title and project code and the full name of the
						funding institution are stated;
					</li>

					<li>
						if a version of the paper was previously presented at
						a conference, the relevant details are given;
					</li>

					<li>
						all reproduced text, equations, figures, data and
						tables are clearly identified and properly referenced;
					</li>

					<li>
						the References section contains only works actually
						cited in the manuscript;
					</li>

					<li>
						any financial or other significant conflict of interest
						that could influence the results or their
						interpretation is disclosed.
					</li>
				</ul>

				<p>
					By submitting a manuscript, authors agree to comply with
					the editorial policy of <em>Theoria</em>.
				</p>
			</div>
		</section>

		<section class="section" id="peer-review">
			<p class="label">PEER REVIEW</p>

			<div class="prose">
				<h2>Double-blind review</h2>

				<p>
					Each manuscript is reviewed by at least two reviewers.
					Reviewers work independently. The identities of reviewers
					are unknown to the authors, and the identities of authors
					are unknown to reviewers.
				</p>

				<p>
					Reviewers are selected according to their relevant
					expertise. They may not be affiliated with the same
					institution as the authors or have been recent co-authors
					of the authors. Author-nominated reviewers are not taken
					into consideration.
				</p>

				<h2>Review process</h2>

				<p>
					Before peer review, the editorial team conducts an initial
					assessment of whether the manuscript is suitable for the
					journal in terms of form and thematic scope.
				</p>

				<p>
					Under normal circumstances, peer review takes no more than
					four weeks. In technically demanding cases it may take up
					to three months. According to the journal’s established
					policy, the period from submission to publication averages
					approximately 90 days.
				</p>

				<h2>Resolving disagreements</h2>

				<p>
					If authors raise serious and well-founded objections to a
					review, the editorial team examines whether the review is
					objective and meets academic standards. If there is doubt
					about its quality or objectivity, additional reviewers are
					appointed.
				</p>

				<p>
					An additional reviewer may also be appointed when the
					reviewers’ recommendations are conflicting or otherwise
					irreconcilable. The final decision on acceptance is made
					exclusively by the Editor-in-Chief.
				</p>
			</div>
		</section>

		<section class="section" id="publication-ethics">
			<p class="label">PUBLICATION ETHICS</p>

			<div class="prose">
				<h2>Ethical standards</h2>

				<p>
					Any individual or institution may report a suspected
					breach of ethical standards or other irregularity to the
					editor or editorial team and provide credible information
					for the purpose of initiating an investigation.
				</p>

				<p>
					During such proceedings, evidence is treated as
					confidential, and persons concerned are given an
					opportunity to respond to the allegations.
				</p>

				<p>
					In handling ethically problematic conduct, the editorial
					team follows the guidelines of the Committee on
					Publication Ethics (COPE).
				</p>

				<h2>Plagiarism</h2>

				<p>
					<em>Theoria</em> does not publish plagiarized work.
					Plagiarism includes taking another person’s ideas or words
					without clear attribution, including verbatim or
					near-verbatim copying and deliberate paraphrasing intended
					to conceal the source.
				</p>

				<p>
					The same applies to equations, data and tables reproduced
					without appropriate attribution. A manuscript showing clear
					indications of plagiarism is rejected. If plagiarism is
					discovered after publication, the article is retracted in
					accordance with the journal’s retraction policy.
				</p>

				<h2>Retraction policy</h2>

				<p>
					A published article may be retracted in cases of
					plagiarism, false authorship, duplicate publication,
					deliberate data manipulation, copyright infringement or
					other serious misuse. Retraction may also be required when
					an article contains serious and numerous errors that cannot
					be adequately corrected by a correction notice.
				</p>

				<p>
					A retraction is published as a separate editorial item.
					In doiSerbia, a link is established between the original
					article and the retraction notice, while the original
					document remains available with its status clearly marked.
				</p>

				<h2>Conflict of interest</h2>

				<p>
					Authors must disclose all potential financial or other
					conflicts of interest relevant to the content of the
					article, the manner in which the research was funded and,
					where applicable, the role of any sponsor in preparing the
					article.
				</p>
			</div>
		</section>

		<section class="section" id="thematic-and-special-issues">
			<p class="label">THEMATIC AND SPECIAL ISSUES</p>

			<div class="prose">
				<h2>Thematic and Special Issues</h2>

				<p>
					Thematic and special issues may be prepared with guest
					editors, who are selected on the basis of appropriate
					scholarly expertise. The Editor-in-Chief and the journal
					retain responsibility for the scholarly and ethical
					standards of every issue.
				</p>

				<p>
					Manuscripts submitted to thematic and special issues
					undergo the same double-blind peer-review process and
					editorial standards as regular submissions. A manuscript
					authored by a guest editor or editorial team member is
					handled independently by another qualified editor, without
					the author taking part in the editorial decision.
				</p>

				<p>
					Contributions by guest editors must not predominate in a
					special issue and may account for no more than 25% of its
					research content.
				</p>
			</div>
		</section>

		<section class="section" id="access-and-rights">
			<p class="label">ACCESS AND RIGHTS</p>

			<div class="prose">
				<h2>Open access</h2>

				<p>
					<em>Theoria</em> is published as an open-access journal.
					All published content is freely available immediately upon
					publication, with no embargo period; no registration or
					subscription is required to read it.
					Published issues are archived in the digital deposit of
					the National Library of Serbia and in the doiSerbia
					Repository.
				</p>

				<h2>Author fees</h2>

				<p>
					The journal does not charge fees for manuscript submission,
					processing or article publication. There are no hidden
					charges.
				</p>

				<h2>Copyright and licensing</h2>

				<p>
					Authors retain copyright over published articles and grant
					the publisher a non-exclusive right to publish and
					distribute the article.
				</p>

				<p>
					Published articles are distributed under the
					<strong>
						Creative Commons Attribution–NonCommercial 4.0
						International licence (CC BY-NC 4.0)
					</strong>.
				</p>

				<p>
					Reuse is permitted under this licence: the work may be
					copied, redistributed, transformed and built upon for
					non-commercial purposes, provided that appropriate credit
					is given, a link to the licence is included and any changes
					are indicated.
				</p>

				<h2>Self-archiving</h2>

				<p>
					Authors may deposit the unreviewed version of the
					manuscript (preprint), the peer-reviewed version
					(postprint) and the published version in institutional or
					subject repositories, on personal websites, institutional
					websites and academic networking platforms.
				</p>

				<p>
					The full bibliographic description of the published article
					should be provided, together with links to the article’s
					DOI and the applicable licence.
				</p>

				<h2>Disclaimer</h2>

				<p>
					The views expressed in published articles do not
					necessarily reflect those of the editor or members of the
					editorial team. Authors assume legal and moral
					responsibility for the ideas expressed in their work.
				</p>
			</div>
		</section>

		<section class="submit" id="send">
			<p class="label">SUBMIT A MANUSCRIPT</p>

			<div class="submit-content">
				<h2>Submission</h2>

				<p>
					Manuscripts prepared in accordance with these instructions
					should be submitted electronically to the journal’s
					editorial office.
				</p>

				<a
					class="email"
					href="mailto:theoria.redakcija@gmail.com"
				>
					theoria.redakcija@gmail.com
				</a>

				<p class="note">
					Before submission, authors are asked to check the technical
					requirements, bibliographic references and information on
					affiliation, funding and possible conflicts of interest.
				</p>
			</div>
		</section>
	</main>
</BaseLayout>